---
import { waHref } from '../data/whatsapp.js';
// VISUAILS — /how-it-works, rewritten. AUDIT-TASK-0.md §F: "REWRITE. 'One
// photo in. A campaign out.' → drop-shaped. All ~24h claims corrected."
//
// §B·3 supplies the frame: "'One photo in' is the per-product story; the batch
// story is 'one folder in'." So the page is shaped around a whole order moving
// through the pipeline, not a single upload.
//
// ── WHAT THE AUGUST 2026 PRICING MODEL CHANGED ON THIS PAGE ────────────────
//
// Section 0 of src/data/pricing.js is the governing document. Two things here
// were falsified by it, and both were structural rather than cosmetic:
//
//   1 · THE PAGE PRESENTED TWO PRODUCTS. "A drop" did one thing and "individual
//       products" did another — a portal against a download link, a reserved
//       window against a queue — and the reader was left to work out which of
//       the two they were buying. That was accurate under the package model,
//       where the buyer picked a size and a service level as two separate
//       questions. It is wrong now. There is ONE thing to order, and the
//       service level follows its size: from WINDOW_THRESHOLD products the
//       order is in the capacity calendar with the reserved window, below it
//       the standard queue. Every place this page used to name two products it
//       now names one line, read from WINDOW_THRESHOLD rather than described,
//       so /pricing and this page cannot give different accounts of the same
//       gate.
//   2 · The scope constant (a Full Drop's 25–30 products) is gone with the
//       package it described. Where the page needed it — the consistency claim
//       — it now says "every product in the order", which is true at any count
//       and does not have to be re-edited when a band moves.
//
// The word "drop" itself is retired from the copy: it means a collection going
// live to the brands we sell to, and this page used it for a work order in its
// own H1. EN says an order or a batch; NL says een bestelling. It survives only
// where a CLIENT's launch is meant, which on this page is nowhere.
//
// Two rules govern every timing claim below:
//   • Task #190 — the ~24-hour promise is retired. Nothing here types a
//     duration; the two sanctioned strings come from turnaround() in
//     pricing.js, one per tier.
//   • The brief's capacity rule — "never promise a delivery date the capacity
//     gate hasn't cleared". The gate is not a footnote on this page, it is
//     step 02 and then a block of its own, because it is the promise that
//     makes every other promise here believable.
//
// The four steps deliberately echo /start rather than reinventing the account:
// /start and /how-it-works describe one pipeline and must not drift apart.
// Where the wording expands here, it expands — it never contradicts.
//
// A CORRECTION TO THAT PARAGRAPH, which used to name "StartPage.astro's `flow`
// array". There is no `flow` array in StartPage.astro. What is there is
// FORM.rail — ['Scope', 'Material', 'Brief', 'Timing', 'Confirm'] — and those
// are the ORDER FORM's five steps, not the service's four. The two lists are
// adjacent, not the same: rail 1–3 all happen inside "you send the products",
// rail 4 is the capacity gate, and rail 5 comes before any production starts.
// So the rule this comment states was pointing at a name that could not be
// checked, which is the same as not pointing anywhere. It names the real thing
// now, and the rule itself is unchanged and still binding.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE FIGURES (task #246). Lucas: the sections lack variation, the page reads
// long, and the site explains its services in words where it should be showing
// them.
//
// This paragraph used to open "measured before this change: 8 sections, 4 of
// them structurally identical to a neighbour, 505 words against 2 images — one
// of which is a hero background". One of those claims is false and it is
// corrected here rather than dropped, because a comment quietly losing a claim
// reads the same as a claim that was always true:
//
//   • "4 structurally identical to a neighbour" — FALSE, and it flattered the
//     change. Signing each section by its layout, container shape then which
//     devices it carries (heading band, card grid, two-column split, figure,
//     CTA band), the page at HEAD had EIGHT distinct signatures and ZERO
//     adjacent repeats. The sections were not repeating each other; they were
//     all prose in a container, which is a different complaint and the one
//     Lucas made. Writing "identical" was me reaching for the DESIGN.md phrase
//     "identical card grids" because it was nearby, not because I had looked.
//
//   • "8 sections" and "2 images, one a hero background" — both true. HEAD
//     referenced exactly two files: /img/banners-04.webp behind the hero, and
//     /img/lifestyle-glow-06.webp, passed to <Compare> as both before AND
//     after. That second one is not a copy-paste slip: the call passes `grade`,
//     and Compare.astro's own header defines grade mode as one photo for both
//     sides with the before auto-desaturated, "for the styles where we have one
//     finished photo, not a separate raw one". /gallery uses it four more
//     times. I had this drafted as a logged defect before reading the
//     component.
//
//   • "505 words" — near enough. A recount gives 482, and the gap is method,
//     not error: this one counts every string in COPY.en of twelve characters
//     or more, a crude floor that keeps class names and hrefs out and takes a
//     few short lines with them. Quoted as "words of visible copy" for that
//     reason, and any comparison has to be run the same way on both sides.
//
// Correcting that one claim produced two more before it landed, and they
// belong in the block whose whole subject is comments outliving the checking
// behind them. I first wrote that HEAD "referenced no images at all", from a
// probe reporting zero — it matched an allow-list of attribute spellings
// (photo=, made=, src=, image=) and HEAD's two references are a CSS url() and
// props called before=/after=, so both read as absence. Then I wrote the
// duplicate before/after up as a defect, which took twelve lines of
// Compare.astro to disprove — twelve lines I had not read when I wrote it.
//
// One shape under all three: a claim about the code, written from something
// adjacent to the code — a phrase from DESIGN.md, a tool's silence, an
// asymmetry that looked wrong. The silence is the dangerous one and the fix is
// filed in rhythm_probe.py: match the DESTINATION, /img/, not the syntax
// around it, because a checker enumerating the spellings it knows reports
// every unknown one as nothing-there, and nothing-there is the answer nobody
// goes back to verify.
//
// What was true and stays true: the page had no explanatory figure of any
// kind. It has five now, and two of them REPLACE prose rather than sitting
// beside it — FigPipeline takes over the four numbered step cards (which also
// retires the 01/02/03/04 scaffolding DESIGN.md rejects), and FigApproval
// takes over the portal's four-item checklist. That is 57 words of prose
// deleted. Section count 8 → 9, adjacent repeats 0 → 0, image files 2 → 4.
//
// The sentence that followed — "adding pictures while keeping every word would
// have made the page longer, which is the opposite of what was asked for" —
// implied the page ended up shorter. It did not, and the same count says so.
// EN, HEAD → now: visible copy 482 → 639. Split by what a reader actually
// meets: alt text 16 → 49, figcaptions 0 → 101, body prose 466 → 489. NL moves
// the same way, 501 → 671, with alt 19 → 46, figcaptions 0 → 109 and body
// prose 482 → 516.
//
// FOUR OF THOSE EIGHT NUMBERS ARE CORRECTIONS, and the correction is worth more
// than the numbers. The first version of this paragraph read "alt text 16 → 56,
// figcaptions 8 → 109, body prose 458 → 474", straight off copy_census.py,
// which at the time sorted a string into a bucket by the SHAPE OF ITS KEY —
// anything matching (alt|aria|label) was accessibility surface, anything
// matching (note|caption) was a figure's note. That is a vocabulary rule
// wearing a heuristic's clothes, and it broke on the next page it met:
// /compare reported 50 words of alt text on a page with no alt text on it, and
// 42 of those 50 are the matrix's visible row headers and a figure's visible
// lane labels, both called `label`. Here it was milder and in the same
// direction — "figcaptions 8" at HEAD, on a revision with no figures in it at
// all, is `portalNote`, a paragraph under the portal section that a suffix
// mistook for a drawing's caption. The census now asks the TEMPLATE which
// attribute a key is bound to, so the buckets follow use rather than naming.
//
// The direction is the part to keep: every one of those errors moved words OUT
// of body prose, which is the column #246 is judged on, so the page read as
// leaner than it was. A tool that is wrong in the flattering direction is one
// nobody has a reason to go back and check.
//
// So the honest position on the half of #246 that says "too much text": body
// prose is close to flat, +23 words EN and +34 NL, and the growth is +101 words
// of figcaption. +33 of the rest is alt text, which no sighted reader meets and
// which five new figures necessarily add.
//
// The +101 is already the trimmed number. The first drafts of these captions
// came to 188 words — a picture, then a paragraph explaining the picture,
// which is the reflex this task exists to answer — and that 188 is the one
// figure on this page nobody can re-measure, because the drafts were never
// committed. Take it as the note it is, not as a number like the others. What
// survives the trim is only what a drawing cannot say about itself, and the
// reasoning for each cut sits above its caption in COPY, where it can be
// argued with.
//
// Anyone re-measuring should split the same three ways before comparing, or a
// page will look like it grew when what grew was its accessibility surface.
//
// All figure copy lives in the COPY table below, in both branches, so
// check_lang_parity holds EN/NL parity on it exactly as it does for prose.
// Drawing constants — ratios, block widths, tile patterns, glyph paths — live
// inside the figure components, because they are the same in every language.
import Compare from './Compare.astro';
// FigFanOut, FigPipeline, FigCapacity, FigApproval and FigFormats used to be
// imported here. They are one component now — see FigFlow.astro's header for
// what each of them claimed and where that claim went. The five files still
// exist and are still imported by nothing; deleting them is a separate,
// reversible decision and this page is not the place to make it.
import FigFlow from './FigFlow.astro';
import {
  TIERS, reviewClaim, turnaround, aftercare, euro, AMOUNT,
  WINDOW_THRESHOLD, vatLabel, vatNote, quote,
} from '../data/pricing.js';
import { localizedPath } from '../i18n/ui.js';

interface Props { lang: 'en' | 'nl'; }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const lp = (p: string) => localizedPath(lang, p);

// The test sample is the only price this page prints, and it now prints with a
// VAT label attached — BRIEF-14's rule, and it was being broken here quietly:
// a bare €0.99 next to "no commitment" reads as the whole of what leaves the
// account, and it is not. Both figures are built here rather than in the copy
// table so neither branch can print one without the other.
const sample = `${euro(AMOUNT.testSample, lang)} ${vatLabel('excl', lang)}`;

// The clip price the flow's video toggle quotes. Same construction as `sample`
// and the same reason: BRIEF-14 forbids a bare figure, and building it here
// means neither language branch can print the number without the label.
const clip = `${euro(AMOUNT.video, 'en')} ${vatLabel('excl', 'en')}`;
const clipNl = `${euro(AMOUNT.video, 'nl')} ${vatLabel('excl', 'nl')}`;

// No `as const` — see PricingPage.astro for why (union-of-tuples breaks .map).
const COPY = {
